good afternoon everybody i'm matt welch  from reason tv  here at the denver state capitol uh  denver is the  site this week of the democratic  national convention i'm here at the  capitol  with a state senator from colorado a  democrat turned republican turned  democrat  and you'll correct me where i'm wrong  senator bob hagedorn  he's been in the legislature here for 24  years  tell us a little bit about your journey  from reagan democrat to republican to  democratica  well actually it was democrat to reagan  democrat slash republican back to the  democratic party  and part of it is that i've always been  very independent and i've  had problems uh back in the 70s  realizing that the democrats seem to be  a little more liberal than i was  and so i figured that you know i kind of  liked some of the stuff that reagan was  saying  but more so i didn't like where the  direction of jimmy carter's presidency  was taking this country  and uh the liberals in the democratic  party so i figured  well i wasn't doing anything miles  become a republican  and uh unfortunately i got engaged in a  at a time when there seemed to be an  assault on  individual rights and freedoms just  beginning in the republican party  starting with a woman's right to choice  and  so very quickly i realized that  i probably didn't exactly fit in the  republican party either  and uh but anyway i wasn't that engaged  at the time in the  80s in politics and kind of dropped out  and uh then late 87 finally decided that  well  the republicans were just moving in to  too far of a direction from where i was  and  at least i knew whether the democrats  were and i felt that i could probably  stomach the liberals better than i could  the  arch conservatives talk a little bit  about your feeling about sort of bush  administration  and even the putative mccain  administration record or approach  towards individual liberty issues that  you seem to care about  well one of the things that basically  since i first campaigned for the  legislature and i was first elected to  the  state house is that i felt my role as  much as anything else were protecting my  constituents  rights liberties and freedoms that has  always been my number one priority  and regardless of whether they're  democrat initiated or republican  initiated  is that i'm not a guy that's keen on  seeing any of our  rights liberties and freedoms taken away  from us and certainly we  uh i mean i like everybody else was just  devastated by 9 11. however uh we  compounded that tragedy with the patriot  act  and we've seen a slow i think decline in  our individual rights liberties and  freedoms  unfortunately a lot of americans seem to  be willing to  give up some of those for the idea of  security  but then where do you draw the line with  government if you do not reign in  government  government will continue to creep along  and  pretty soon you can wake up one morning  and hey you have no rights liberties or  freedoms  and that's what my main concern is uh  certainly with uh  uh you know there's there's a senator  mccain who's running for the presidency  in 2008  who seems to be a little different from  the senator mccain from 2000  kind of like the guy from 2000 but right  now it looks like it's going to be  another four years of  of another eight years of the bush  administration particularly  slow deterioration of our individual  freedoms which  you know it still seems incredibly  ironic considering it's the republicans  who historically you could kind of trust  at times  to protect your rights liberties and  freedoms but you know only in certain  arenas just like the democrats  only in limited arenas is there a sort  of  libertarian flavored backlash in the  mountain west particularly against the  republican party  you hear some sort of belly hood  movement in that direction but does it  actually tangibly exist do you think  well i would say very much so if you  looked at the west i think you will find  that the west is  easily more libertarian than the midwest  or the east  and one of the things is that i thought  you know  for a long time kind of the hallmark of  particularly the republican party in  colorado was  a real protection of individual freedoms  and i think that what we've seen now is  that  the democrats we have majorities in the  house and senate  as well as of course the governor but we  have elected  we have democrats in seats that have  very well performing republican numbers  but the problem is  is that the republicans have nominated  candidates who literally want to take  away people's rights  i mean very significantly abortion  rights is uh  area that resonates very well and  we have places like jefferson county  that  you know we hold the majority of state  elected offices  house and senate legislative offices in  jefferson county  jefferson county should be predominantly  republican in terms of their  representation down here but they have  chosen to go  with shall we say evangelical republican  candidates  which infringes upon i think an overall  uh level of uh  respect for freedoms and into you know  in our rights and so i think that uh the  republicans by having moving away from  barry goldwater  into this more evangelical direction  has definitely cost them support in the  western states  because you know we appreciate  individual rights liberties and freedoms  and republicans have been sensitive to  that and enjoyed those rights  and now their own candidates are saying  well you know we're going to take these  away from you  that's great uh from the denver state  house i'm with  senator bob hagedorn this has been matt  welch reason tv thank you very much
 

